	title: 'Colours as code',
	spec: 'src/common/colors.ts is the theme: one exported object whose top-level fields are either plain values (fgL: 0.85, fgC: 0.02, ...) or functions computing a style from them at call time (assistant: () => ({ fg: [colors.fgL, colors.fgC, 55] })). local.ts overrides a top-level field (colors.fgL = 0.9) and everything derived follows. No string references like $fgL and no colors.ason. The terminal turns styles into escape codes; the host generates CSS from the same object for the web page, which never sees the functions. A light/dark choice, if any, is a setting. Reference for the palette: the old colors.ason and colors.ts (artifacts); keep its look, not its format. Every colour obeys the readable-text invariant of tasks/README.md: colors.screen is the lightest dark background designed for, and a test measures every text part of every style against its own bg (else screen), and against linkBg, at 4.5:1 (3:1 for cursor marks; page.border 3:1 on the canvas). Secondary text (tool glimpses, the `$ command` beside a description, block ids, placeholders, hints, table rules, code fences, quote bars, in-flight markers, inbox tags) is oklch.quiet(fg, bg): a lightness step darker, never under 4.6:1, drawn by ansi.quiet, which returns to the style fg after. The terminal never uses SGR 2 (faint); the web never fades resting text by opacity: host/web.ts emits --quiet per style (the page\'s measured on its button surface) and the CSS uses it.',
